"Orbital Resonances | VirtualDemos",
"Orbital Resonances",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Asteroid belt: {#}:{#} resonance; {#}:{#} resonance; {#}:{#} resonance; Between the gaps; Hildas at {#}:{#}; Whole belt; Clearing the gaps. Kuiper belt: Pluto; Pluto’s orbit, bad timing; Plutinos; Classical belt; Whole Kuiper belt. Finding Neptune: Uranus alone; Le Verrier’s planet; The real Neptune; Hunt for it yourself.",
"<strong>Supplementary: Clearing the gaps.</strong> This setup computes {#} simulated asteroids ({#} on a phone) pulled by the Sun, Jupiter and Saturn, and shows over the real counts how many are still at each distance and where the removed ones started. Run it for {#} years or more. Which resonances lose asteroids first, how much emptier do they get, and does the {#}:{#} gap open in that time?",
"There are three scenes. Asteroid belt follows asteroids pulled by the Sun and Jupiter; Kuiper belt follows icy bodies beyond Neptune pulled by the Sun and Neptune; Finding Neptune replays the sightings of Uranus from {#} to {#}.",
"In the first two scenes the large view shows the orbits, in 3D or flat from above. Each time the planet passes the body, a yellow dot marks the place on the body’s orbit where it happened. “Turns with the planet” turns the picture with the planet, so the planet stays still and the pattern of the passes is easy to see.",
"The card on the picture sets the body’s starting distance from the Sun \\(a\\) in astronomical units (AU; {#} AU is Earth’s average distance from the Sun), the starting eccentricity \\(e\\) ({#} is a circle; the bigger \\(e\\), the more stretched the orbit) and where it starts compared with the planet. It also shows the period ratio: how many times the body goes round for each turn of the planet, or the other way round beyond Neptune.",
"Below the view, a bar chart shows the real numbers of known asteroids (or Kuiper belt objects) at each distance from the Sun, counted from the JPL Small Body Database, with the resonances marked. Each simulated body adds a dot at its starting distance, as high as the largest eccentricity it has reached. Click a bar to start a body at that distance. A second chart follows the body’s eccentricity (asteroid belt) or its distance from Neptune (Kuiper belt) over time.",
"An asteroid whose orbit stretches until it reaches in to Mars’s orbit is taken out of the run, since a close pass by Mars or Earth would soon throw it off its path. A body that comes within the planet’s own sphere of control (its Hill radius) is thrown out too.",
"In Finding Neptune, the chart shows how far Uranus was seen from where the best possible orbit puts it, in arcseconds (″; {#}″ make one degree). Planet X is an extra planet you can add; its distance, mass and position change the fit right away.",
"The real gaps in the asteroid belt took millions of years to clear, much too long to run here. The demo runs a few thousand years and shows the kicks that clear them.",
"Record reading stores the setup, the starting distance, the period ratio, the time run and what happened. Export to worksheet sends the readings to the matching Data table below; Clear starts a new series, which goes into a new copy of the table.",
"Find out why the asteroid belt has empty lanes at certain distances from the Sun, why Pluto never hits Neptune even though their orbits cross, and how Neptune was found from its pull on Uranus before anyone saw it.",
"<strong>Kepler’s third law.</strong> A planet farther from the Sun takes longer to go round. Measured in years and AU, the period \\(P\\) and the average distance \\(a\\) follow \\(P^2 = a^3\\). Jupiter, at {#} AU, takes {#} years.",
"<strong>Resonance.</strong> An asteroid at {#} AU takes {#} years to go round, exactly one third of Jupiter’s {#}. Every time Jupiter goes round once, the asteroid goes round three times, so it passes Jupiter twice per Jupiter orbit, at two places on opposite sides of the Sun, and then at the same two places again and again. The tugs from those passes repeat the same pattern every time, so they add up, like pushes on a swing given at the same point of every swing, and the orbit grows more and more stretched. This is called a {#}:{#} resonance. An asteroid at most other distances meets Jupiter at a different place each time, so the tugs point every which way and average out.",
"<strong>The Kirkwood gaps.</strong> In {#} Daniel Kirkwood noticed that almost no asteroids have periods that are simple fractions of Jupiter’s. Today more than a million asteroids are known, and the gaps are plain to see in a chart of their distances.",
"<strong>The outer solar system.</strong> Beyond Neptune is the Kuiper belt, a ring of icy bodies. Pluto goes round the Sun twice while Neptune goes round three times (a {#}:{#} resonance). Its orbit is stretched enough to come closer to the Sun than Neptune does.",
"<strong>Finding Neptune.</strong> Uranus was found in {#}. By {#} astronomers could not make any orbit fit both the old sightings and the new ones. In {#} Urbain Le Verrier worked out where an unseen planet must be to pull Uranus off course. Johann Galle pointed a telescope there on {#} September {#} and found Neptune within one degree of the predicted spot.",
"An asteroid in the {#}:{#} resonance goes round five times while Jupiter goes round twice. Jupiter takes {#} years. How long does the asteroid take to go round once?",
"When you push a child on a swing, why does it work best to push at the same point of every swing? What would happen if you pushed at a random point each time?",
"Pluto’s orbit crosses Neptune’s. Make a guess before you measure: could Pluto and Neptune ever collide?",
"<strong>Part {#}: kicks in the asteroid belt.</strong> Choose the {#}:{#} resonance setup and run it. Watch where Jupiter passes the asteroid (the yellow dots) and what happens to the asteroid’s orbit. When the run stops because the asteroid’s orbit reaches Mars’s, record a reading. Then do the same for the {#}:{#} resonance. For the {#}:{#} resonance, Between the gaps and Hildas at {#}:{#}, run each for about {#} years and record a reading. Last, choose the {#}:{#} resonance again, move the starting distance to {#} AU, run for about {#} years and record a reading. Send the six readings to the worksheet, then clear them.",
"<strong>Part {#}: where the gaps are.</strong> For each row of the Gaps table, work out where the resonance should be from Kepler’s third law, as in Analysis {#}. Then find the gap in the bar chart of real asteroids and write down its distance from the Sun (click the shortest bar in the gap; the starting distance on the card shows its distance).",
"<strong>Part {#}: Pluto and Neptune.</strong> Switch to the Kuiper belt and choose Pluto. Run it for about {#} years and record a reading. Then choose Pluto’s orbit, bad timing: the same orbit, but starting in a different place compared with Neptune. Run it until the run stops and record a reading. Last, choose Classical belt, run it for about {#} years and record a reading. Send the three readings to the worksheet, then clear them.",
"<strong>Part {#}: finding Neptune.</strong> Switch to Finding Neptune. Choose Uranus alone and record a reading, then Le Verrier’s planet and record a reading. Then choose Hunt for it yourself and set planet X to {#} AU and {#} Earth masses. Record a reading with planet X at each of these positions on {#} September {#}: {#}°, {#}°, {#}°, {#}°, {#}° and {#}°. Last, choose The real Neptune and record a reading. Send the readings to the worksheet.",
