"Pluto’s closest point to the Sun is {#} AU and Neptune’s orbit is at {#} AU. From Part {#}, how close did Pluto ever come to Neptune? What happened when the same orbit started with bad timing? Was your guess in Before the lab {#} right?",
"In Part {#}, how big was the leftover drift with Uranus alone, and with the best planet X you found? At which position on {#} September {#} was the drift smallest? Compare it with the position of the real Neptune (show the real Neptune, or choose The real Neptune setup).",
"The demo’s orbits come from Newton’s law of gravity, so a repeated run with the same settings gives exactly the same numbers. The uncertainty is in what the model leaves out: each asteroid feels only the Sun and one planet, all the orbits lie flat in one plane, and a run lasts thousands of years, not the hundreds of millions the real belt has had. Name one of these and say how it might change your Part {#} results. In Part {#}, planet X moves on a fixed orbit and its pull on Jupiter and Saturn is left out, so even the real Neptune leaves a little drift. How big is it, compared with the drift with Uranus alone?",
"Earth has about {#}/{#} of Jupiter’s mass, and there is no gap in the asteroid belt at a resonance with Earth. Why not?",
"Saturn’s rings also have gaps, the largest being the Cassini Division. Using what you saw here, what kind of object could clear a gap in a ring?",
"Le Verrier’s planet was too far from the Sun and too heavy, yet Galle still found Neptune within a degree of the predicted spot. From your Part {#} trials, which property of planet X did the leftover drift depend on most?",
"Some astronomers think an undiscovered “Planet Nine” far beyond Neptune lines up the orbits of the most distant Kuiper belt objects. How is that idea like Le Verrier’s hunt for Neptune?",
"Prelab answers, the Data, Gaps, Pluto and Neptune, and Finding Neptune tables, the answers to the analysis and uncertainty sections, and answers to the questions. Identify simulated data.",
